# Is economics a topic or a method?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What do the price of bread, Becker's theory of crime and the tip you leave a waiter have in common? Four quick answers to what economics is — and why this course starts with questions instead"
next: {"title":"Why use models that are obviously false?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/04-why-use-models-that-are-obviously-false.cast"}

## Bread, a burglar and a tip
use: venn_diagram
with:
    mode: containment
    universe_box: false
    sets:
      - {label: The economy, only_label: The economy}
      - {label: 'Method: scarce choices'}
sources:
    - id: marshall1890
      title: Principles of Economics
      authors: Alfred Marshall
      year: 1890
      finding: Economics is 'a study of mankind in the ordinary business of life'.
    - id: robbins1932
      title: An Essay on the Nature and Significance of Economic Science
      authors: Lionel Robbins
      year: 1932
      finding: Economics studies human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means which have alternative uses (p. 15).

    card title "Is economics a topic or a method?"

    hide set_label_0

What do the price of bread, a burglar's choice and the tip you leave a waiter have in common?
    icon bread size 80 x 500 y 520 icon_look picture of bread
    icon burglar size 100 x 230 y 520 or ["detective","money bag"] icon_look picture of ninja
    icon tip size 100 x 770 y 520 or ["money"] icon_look picture of coin
    parallel true

In Harbourtown, Mia sells a loaf at a price, a burglar eyes the little bank, and a diner leaves c (@highlight tip@)oins on a saucer.
    highlight bread

B: Only the bread is economics, surely. The other two belong to criminologists and etiquette columns.
    highlight bread effect circle

    quiz "Which of these is economics?"
        right: By one famous answer, all three are. By an older one, only the bread.
        wrong: Look at what the burglar and the diner are doing: each weighs one thing against another.
        * Only the bread
        * Bread and the tip: money changes hands
        + All three

That depends on what economics is. And it matters: it decides which questions economists claim to answer, from prison sentences to pensions.
    highlight burglar tip effect pulse

One old answer: in 1890 Alfred Marshall called it a study of mankind in the ordinary business of life.
    draw set_0 region_label_a

B: So it's a topic, like geology: markets, money, jobs. My loaf goes in; the burglar stays out.
    move bread to.x 500 to.y 360 duration 1.2

In 1932 Lionel Robbins drew a bigger circle: human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means.
    portrait robbins cites ["robbins1932"] of "Lionel Robbins" cameo true

    erase robbins

That makes economics a method. Wherever someone gives up one thing to get another, it applies.
    draw set_1 set_label_1

B: Then the burglar is in: loot against prison. And the diner to (@move tip to.x 620 to.y 480 duration 1.2@)o: a few coins against a waiter's frown.
    move burglar to.x 380 to.y 480 duration 1.2

That's the common thread: all three are choices where something has to be given up.
    highlight bread burglar tip

    pause 0.4

By topic, only the bread is economics. By meth (@highlight set_1@)od, all three are, and economists have studied every one.
    highlight set_0

## Becker's parking ticket
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: E(p) = F*p
    variable: p
    params:
      F: {value: 50, min: 20, max: 150, step: 5, label: fine F ($)}
      g: {value: 10, fixed: true, label: gain g ($)}
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 1
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 60
    x_label: chance of a ticket
    y_label: expected fine ($)
    editable:
      - F
    marks:
      - {kind: hline, at: g, label: gain $10, id: gain}
      - {kind: point, at: g/F, x_label: break-even, id: be}
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 150
      w: 700
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: becker1992
      title: The Economic Way of Looking at Life (Nobel lecture)
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1992
      finding: Late for an oral exam, Becker weighed the chance and size of a parking fine against a paid lot, parked on the street, and got no ticket; this started his thinking on crime.
    - id: becker1968
      title: 'Crime and Punishment: An Economic Approach'
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1968
      finding: Offenders respond to the probability and size of punishment; optimal policy can trade fines against costly enforcement. Journal of Political Economy 76(2).
    - id: becker1973
      title: 'A Theory of Marriage: Part I'
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1973
      finding: Marriage analysed as a market. Journal of Political Economy 81(4).
    - id: hamermesh1974
      title: An Economic Theory of Suicide
      authors: Daniel S. Hamermesh, Neal M. Soss
      year: 1974
      finding: Suicide modelled as a choice depending on expected lifetime utility. Journal of Political Economy 82(1).
    - id: nobel1992
      title: The Prize in Economic Sciences 1992
      authors: Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences
      year: 1992
      finding: Becker, 'for having extended the domain of microeconomic analysis to a wide range of human behaviour and interaction, including nonmarket behaviour'.

    portrait becker cites ["becker1992"] of "Gary Becker" cameo true hidden true
    cards mathcards options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after_math"}] then after_math hidden true
        * Show me the math
        * Skip it
    scratch mb work [{"tex":"g > p\\cdot F"},{"tex":"p^* = g/F = 10/50 = 0.2"},{"tex":"2F:\\ p^* = 10/100 = 0.1"}] x 850 y 470 font_size 24 hidden true
    scratch emp cites ["becker1968","becker1973","hamermesh1974","nobel1992"] work ["crime · 1968","marriage · 1973","suicide · 1974","Nobel · 1992"] x 850 y 470 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Becker's parking ticket"

B: The boldest user of that method was Gary Becker, and it began with a parking space.
    draw becker

B: Late for a student's oral exam, he had to choose: a paid lot, or the street and the risk of a ticket.
    draw axes

What did he weigh?
    erase becker

B: Say the lot costs ten dollars and a ticket fifty: illustrative numbers. The street saves him ten.
    draw gain gain_label

    pause 0.3

B: The expected fine is the chance of a ticket times the fine, so it climbs along this line.
    draw curve_0 eq

So where the line crosses ten, at one chance in five, the street stops paying.
    draw be be_x_label

    pause 0.5

B: He judged the chance was lower, took the street, and got no ticket. Then he wondered whether criminals weigh things the same way.
    point at.ref be gesture circle

Try it: raise the fine, and watch the break-even chance slide left.
    explore

B: Double the fine, and one chance in ten deters like one in five did. His 1968 paper argued that big fines can stand in for costly police.
    animate params.F 100 duration 3 ghost ["curve_0","be"]

    draw mathcards

    ask "Want the two-line algebra behind the break-even point?" on mathcards stage own after clear

@math

    hide mathcards_1 mathcards_2

Park illegally when the gain beats the chance times the fine.
    draw mb_box mb_line_1

So the break-even chance is the gain over the fine: ten over fifty, a fifth.
    draw mb_line_2

Double the fine and it halves, to a tenth.
    draw mb_line_3

    erase mb

@after_math

    hide mathcards_1 mathcards_2

The method has a second half: equilibrium. Police respond to (@highlight curve_0@) crime, offenders respond to polic (@highlight gain@)e, and the crime rate settles where those p (@point at.ref be gesture circle@)lans fit, just like a bread price.

B: Then Becker took it into marriag (@draw emp_line_2@)e and children, and Hamermesh and Soss even mod (@draw emp_line_3@)elled suicide as a choice.
    draw emp_box emp_line_1

His 1992 Nobel prize cited nonmarket behaviour. Fans and critics alike call it economic imperialism.
    draw emp_line_4

## The tip
sources:
    - id: lynn1990
      title: 'Restaurant tipping: an examination of three ''rational'' explanations'
      authors: Michael Lynn, Andrea Grassman
      year: 1990
      finding: 'Interviews with departing diners: tips fit buying social approval and equity, not buying future service. Journal of Economic Psychology 11(2).'
    - id: lynn1993
      title: 'Consumer tipping: a cross-country study'
      authors: Michael Lynn, George M. Zinkhan, Judy Harris
      year: 1993
      finding: 'Using a tipping guide covering about 30 countries, the US had the most tipped service professions: 31 of 33. Journal of Consumer Research 20.'
    - id: nagin2013
      title: Deterrence in the Twenty-First Century
      authors: Daniel S. Nagin
      year: 2013
      finding: Certainty of apprehension, not severity of punishment, is the more effective deterrent. Crime and Justice 42.
    - id: gneezy2000
      title: A Fine is a Price
      authors: Uri Gneezy, Aldo Rustichini
      year: 2000
      finding: Haifa day-care centres that fined late parents saw late pickups rise, and stay high after the fine was removed. Journal of Legal Studies 29(1).
    - id: madrian2001
      title: 'The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior'
      authors: Brigitte C. Madrian, Dennis F. Shea
      year: 2001
      finding: Automatic enrolment sharply raised pension plan participation. Quarterly Journal of Economics 116(4).

    icon coin size 120 x 160 y 430 icon_look picture of coin hidden true
    box n_topic "Topic: the economy" radius 12 in answers hidden true
    box n_rc "Rational choice: tip $0" radius 12 in answers hidden true
    box n_beh "Behavioural: guilt, fairness" radius 12 in answers hidden true
    box n_inst "Institutional: local rules" radius 12 in answers hidden true
    grid answers walk true fit.h 480 fit.w 650 fit.x 320 fit.y 160 hidden true
    text readout "US: 31 of 33 jobs" cites ["lynn1993"] x 165 y 280 font_size 24 hidden true
    text nagin "certainty > severity" cites ["nagin2013"] x 165 y 280 font_size 24 hidden true
    text stamp "CONTESTED" x 165 y 590 font_size 32 hidden true
    mark stamp_box around stamp kind box hidden true

    card title "The tip"

B: If choice explains a burglar, try the tip. You eat once in a town you'll never see agai (@highlight coin effect circle@)n. Why leave anything?
    draw coin

Pure self-interest says you shouldn't: the waiter can't punish you, and the meal is already served.
    draw n_rc

B: Yet people tip anyway. And when researchers asked diners leaving restaurants, buying better service next time didn't explain the tips.
    fade n_rc to 0.35

Behavioural economists keep the choice but change the chooser: we feel guilt, want approval, care about fair shares.
    draw n_beh

B: But the guilt isn't the same everywhere. One study of thirty countries found tips expected in thirty-one of thirty-three American service jobs, the most of any.
    draw readout

That's the institutional answer: a tip is a rule of the place, built up over history, and choices are made inside it. In Tokyo, a restaurant tip (@highlight n_inst@) is usually not expected.
    draw n_inst

    erase readout

B: Couldn't a rational-choice economist just call guilt a cost?
    highlight n_beh

Yes, but a theory that can absorb any behaviour risks predicting none. How far the  (@draw stamp stamp_box ends@)method reaches is contested.

Incentives do matter: the chance of being caught deters crime far more rel (@draw nagin@)iably than the size of the punishment. So do norms and rules.

B: So which is it, a topic or a method?
    erase nagin

    fade n_rc to 1

Four answers, then: a topic, a method of choice an (@highlight n_topic n_rc n_beh n_inst@)d equilibrium, a chooser with feelings, and rules that shape the choice. Each lights up some things and misses others.
    draw n_topic

    quiz "A city doubles its parking fine but halves its patrols, so a ticket is half as likely. In Becker's simplest model, what happens to illegal parking?"
        right: Half the chance times twice the fine is the same expected fine. Real drivers may still react more to the patrols.
        wrong: Multiply the chance by the fine, before and after.
        * It falls
        * It rises
        + It stays about the same

    quiz "Day-care centres in Haifa started fining parents who came late. Late pickups rose, and stayed up after the fine was dropped. What fits best?" voice b
        right: Guilt kept parents on time; a small fine bought it off, and the old norm didn't come back.
        wrong: Before the fine, what made parents hurry?
        * Parents didn't notice the fine
        + The fine turned a duty into a price
        * Fines never change behaviour

    hide coin answers stamp stamp_box

    cards sortq
        Rational choice: Speed cameras
        Behavioural: Pension default
        Rational choice: Cigarette tax
        Institutional: Sunday trading law

    ask "Which answer explains each finding most simply? Sort the cards." on sortq store srt stage own
        right: All {srt.total} sorted.
        wrong: You got {srt} of {srt.total}. Prices and penalties point to rational choice; defaults to behaviour; laws to institutions.

That's why this course starts with questions, not a method: th (@highlight n_topic n_rc n_beh n_inst@)e question decides which tool to pick up.
    show coin answers stamp stamp_box

